A||||5||black-86-GT||engine removal ||||4||
Z||000000||black-86-GT||06-10-2000||06:38 PM||||I was wondering how hard it would be to take out the engine. I dont have a lift and only have the basic tools what would it take for me to remove it. It looks like it would be a nightmare. thanx all||||reg||4
Z||000001||2birds||06-10-2000||07:46 PM||||The engine comes out from the bottom, by dropping the rear cradle with the transaxle and engine still attached to it.  Maybe do-able without a lift, but a lot easier with one.  Haynes and/or Chilton's manual shows how to lift only the back with a 4X4 piece of wood and a jack.<P>Anybody that can remove it from the top has my vote for President.||||reg||1
Z||000002||BN Boomer||06-10-2000||10:07 PM||||How are you going to vote for all of us that have removed an engine from the top of a Fiero?   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>In fact, here's mine currently in the middle of having it engine replaced using the death from above method...<P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/gdubs_suv_stuff/vc-013f.jpg">  <P>I personally don't think pulling the engine using either method is that difficult, but I am also fairly mechanically inclined and probably have more tools than typical. I've pulled Fiero engines both ways and they both have their advantages. Even if you drop it out the bottom, you still have to seperate the engine from the transmission and cradle and will thus need a lift or hoist of some type, even it's just a come-along hooked to the rafters or some strong buddies. <p>[This message has been edited by BN Boomer (edited 06-10-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||wkayl||06-11-2000||01:08 AM||||Thanks for posting that picture.  Now my wife kinda believes me when I say that all real men's garages look like that.  In a state of organized confusion.||||reg||1
Z||000004||prof bobo||06-13-2000||05:04 PM||||Fiero Engine Removal  <P>This procedure varies somewhat from the Pontiac and Haynes manuals, but it's quicker (about 2 hours), easier and doesn't<BR>require discharging/recharging A/C and realigning rear wheels. <P>Check off each item as done: <P>     Raise front of car 6-8" (Put on ramps), remove trunk side louvre covers. <BR>     Drain oil & coolant. <BR>     Disconnect battery, remove battery and plastic protector (13mm & 9/32"). <BR>     Remove trunk lid ground strap & lite connector, then lid (13mm). <BR>     Remove rear console, disconnect ECM harness from ECM, push harness through firewall to engine. <BR>     Disconnect all engine ground straps, battery harness connector block & fusible links (10mm & 1/4"). <BR>     Disconnect upper radiator hose at thermostat, heater hoses at water pump. <BR>     Remove rear torque reaction strut bolt, tip up strut (15mm). <BR>     Remove intake flex duct and PCV elbow, disconnect air temp sensor, remove air filter. <BR>     Remove fuel pump relay (leave connected to harness) (1/4"). <BR>     Disconnect throttle cable and cruise control cable (15mm). <BR>     Disconnect vacuum hoses, label as necessary. <BR>     Disconnect fuel lines at engine. <BR>     Disconnect clutch slave cylinder, shifter cables w/support 'S' bracket (13mm & 15mm). <BR>     Remove rear trunk heat shield (1/4"). <BR>     Raise rear of car, put on blocks. <BR>     Disconnect lower radiator hoses at pipes. <BR>     Remove front trunk heat shield (10mm). <BR>     Disconnect A/C wiring, remove A/C compressor don't discharge, support out of way (15mm). <BR>     Remove rear wheels, remove fender splash guards. <BR>     Disconnect main parking brake cable from cradle, each wheel cable (save spring). <BR>     Remove and suspend brake calipers w/hoses intact (detach brake hose clamps at strut), remove rotors (T-45 & 10mm).<BR>     Remove upper strut nuts and side louvre brackets. <BR>     Loosen, but don't remove cradle bolts. Cradle will fall otherwise! (16mm & 17mm) <BR>     Lower car till cradle is resting on dolly. <BR>     Remove cradle bolts. <BR>     Raise car (jack and block, jack and block) about 3', until cradle can be rolled out. Watch for interference! <BR>     Disconnect O2 sensor, remove exhaust Y-pipe, EGR valve and heat shield (12mm & 10mm). <BR>     Disconnect radiator intermediate pipe (10mm & 15mm). <BR>     Remove starter, remove sheet metal clutch cover (15mm & 10mm). <BR>     Disconnect speedometer sensor. <BR>     Disconnect engine mounts (15mm) <BR>     Disconnect transaxle, pry only at cast boss at upper rear (18mm). <BR>     Lift engine. <P>  <BR>  Protect CV joint boots! <BR>  Keep axles supported while on cradle so inner CV joints don't separate. <BR>  Discard old exhaust component bolts. <P><BR>  <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||MikeE||06-17-2000||09:31 AM||||I learned this method while talking to Lee Kiely on the phone (he made a crude, but  inexpensive, Quad4 swap kit a few years back).  It's faily easy but does require a hydraulic engine hoist.  As the manuals say, you need something to support the cradle.  Lee made a custom device with a steel plate and casters but I just found an old wooden fork lift pallet and bolted on some casters from Sears, who also suplied the chain and angle brackets.  Anyway, after I disconnect all the necessary engine parts and the brake calipers, E-brake, etc. I lower the car (with a hyd jack)enough to support the cradle on the homemade platform.  Then I remove the cradle mounting bolts and unbolt the struts at the upper end.<P>I then raise the car just enough so that I can bolt the angle bracket to the strut mounting holes (I drilled the bracket to allow bolting to 2 holes).  I attach a chain to the bracket, hook the chain with the hoist, and lift the body high enough to roll the entire assembly out-cradle, engine, trans, axles, struts, and all.  The beauty of this is that you don't have to disconnect the lower struts and mess up the alignment (unless the struts are being changed) or deal with removing the axles.  Even if you are working on the trans and/or axles, access is easier when they're out of the car.  The back end is light with all these pieces removed so it's a cinch to raise it.<P>This method could probably be done with a floor jack and 4x4s but it would probably be a pain.  The rear end has to go fairly high to clear the struts.  Of course, you can disconnect the axles and leave them in the car but if you are just putting in a new clutch or doing something with the engine, why bother?  Anyway, this is what I do, but everyone has their own method.||||reg||1
